<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · deity</p><h1 class="enc-title">Demeter</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Demeter was the Greek goddess of grain, agriculture and the fertility of the earth, and mother of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persephone">Persephone</a>. The myth of Persephone's abduction by Hades and Demeter's search for her explained the cycle of the seasons and lay at the heart of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eleusinian-mysteries">Eleusinian Mysteries</a>, the most famous mystery cult of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>. Her sanctuaries were found throughout the Greek world, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> identified her with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ceres">Ceres</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/demeter.jpg" alt="Demeter" width="320" height="733" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CC BY 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Demeter_Altemps_Inv8546.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
